# Code Organization and Structure Rules

## Project Structure Patterns

### 1. Module Organization

```
service/src/app-models/[module]/
├── actions/              # Individual action files
├── [module]-app-model.ts
├── [module]-manifest.ts
├── setup-[module]-rest-api.ts
└── setup-[module]-store.ts
```

### 2. Action File Structure

- Create separate files for each action in `actions/` directory
- Use descriptive names: `[action-name]-action.ts`
- Export a single action per file following this pattern:
  ```typescript
  export const ActionName: RequestAction<ActionType> = async ({ injector, getBody }) => {
    // Implementation
  }
  ```

### 3. Import Organization

- Group imports logically:
  1. External framework imports
  2. Internal common imports
  3. Local module imports
- Use consistent import naming
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase

## Frontend Structure Patterns

### 1. Component Organization

```
frontend/src/
├── components/
│   ├── routes/          # Route definitions
│   ├── [module]/        # Module-specific components
│   └── shared/          # Shared components
├── pages/               # Page components
├── services/            # Business logic services
└── utils/               # Utility functions
```

### 2. Service Layer Pattern

- Use injectable services for business logic
- Follow singleton pattern for stateful services
- Implement proper error handling and logging
- Use observables for reactive state management

### 3. Page Component Structure

- Use Shade component pattern consistently
- Implement proper lifecycle management
- Handle loading and error states
- Follow accessibility best practices

## Development Workflow

### 1. Feature Development Order

1. Define API endpoints in common package
2. Regenerate schemas
3. Implement backend actions
4. Create/update frontend routes
5. Implement UI components
6. Write E2E tests
7. Update documentation

### 2. Testing Patterns

- Create E2E tests for complete user flows
- Test both happy path and error scenarios
- Use descriptive test names and descriptions
- Follow existing test patterns and helpers

### 3. Error Handling Strategy

- Use proper HTTP status codes
- Log errors with appropriate context
- Implement graceful degradation
- Provide user-friendly error messages

## Best Practices

### 1. Code Quality

- Follow existing naming conventions
- Use TypeScript strictly (avoid `any`)
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Keep functions focused and small

### 2. Documentation

- Update README when adding new features
- Document complex business logic
- Maintain API documentation
- Keep cursor rules up to date

### 3. Performance Considerations

- Use lazy loading for heavy components
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Optimize bundle size
- Monitor performance metrics

### 4. Security Practices

- Validate all user input
- Use proper authentication/authorization
- Sanitize data appropriately
- Follow security best practices

## Refactoring Guidelines

### 1. When to Refactor

- When adding similar functionality
- When patterns become inconsistent
- When code becomes difficult to maintain
- When performance issues arise

### 2. Refactoring Process

- Extract common patterns into reusable utilities
- Follow established architectural patterns
- Maintain backward compatibility when possible
- Update tests and documentation

### 3. Code Review Focus Areas

- Consistency with existing patterns
- Proper error handling
- Security considerations
- Performance implications
- Test coverage

## Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Direct DOM manipulation instead of using framework patterns
- Inline route definitions instead of using route files
- Manual hash manipulation instead of using navigateToRoute
- Custom validation instead of using framework validation
- Exposing internal errors to users
- Inconsistent error handling patterns
- Not following established module structure
